1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Absalon Hvide''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Biskop i Roskilde 1158-1191. Ärkebiskop av Lund ca. februari-mars 1178 - 1201-03-21
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Företrädare'''
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/people/Eskil-Christiernsen/6000000043381791892 Eskil Christiernsen]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Efterträdare'''
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/people/Andreas-Sunesen-Hvide-Galen/6000000017096045235 Andreas Sunesen]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biskop Absalon grundlagde ikke København. Flere fund viser, at byen allerede i 1000-tallet havde en stormandsgård, kirke og handelsplads. Byen klarede sig fint uden Absalon, siger arkæologer.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absalon http://www.soroeklosterkirke.dk/kirkens_historie.html død link
2 CONT
2 CONT Absalon blev biskop i Roskilde i 1158 på foranledning af den nykårede konge Valdemar. I denne egenskab omdannede han benediktinerklosteret i Sorø, som faderen havde grundlagt, til et velstående cistercienserkloster med en klosterk
2 CONC irke, der som den eneste del af klosteret er bevaret intakt. I Sorø Akademi, der er indrettet i resterne af de tidligere klosterbygninger, findes rester af murværk fra klostertiden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han fik i 1167 landsbyen Havn (der senere blev til København) af kongen, hvor han byggede en borg på en holm ude i vandet. Rester af Absalons Borg ligger stadig under Christiansborg. (Det er kun en myte at han grundlagde København
2 CONC ).
2 CONT
2 CONT Absalon var sammen med Valdemar den Store med til at indtage vendernes Rügen og dele af Pommern i 1169. Han blev i 1178 ærkebiskop i Lund efter Ærkebiskop Eskil, og indledte med at virke for, at den kanoniske ret efterlevedes, men
2 CONC  kom derfor snart i åben konfrontation med folket i det østensundske Danmark. I 1180-1182 kuede han med velvillig bistand af de skånelandske stormænd på den mest brutale vis de skånelandske bønders oprør mod hans politik. En polit
2 CONC ik, der bl. a. gik ud på indførelse af bispetiende. Efter at Knud 6. var blevet konge i 1182, var Absalon i realiteten den, der styrede Danmark. Ifølge et gammelt sagn af Saxo blev Absalon født da hans fader Asser Rig var i vender
2 CONC nes land. Asser Rig havde, inden han rejste fra sin gravide kone, sagt at hvis hun således fødte en pige mens han var borte skulle der bygges et spir på Fjenneslev Kirke, som han var i færd med at bygge, men hvis der blev født en 
2 CONC dreng skulle der et rigtigt tårn på kirken. Da Asser Rig senere kom hjem, var der to tårne på Kirken, han havde fået tvillinger (Esbern Snarre og Absalon).
2 CONT
2 CONT Danmarks Adels Aarbog, Thiset, Hiort-Lorenzen, Bobé, Teisen., (Dansk Adelsforening), [1884 - 2005]., DAA 1898:225.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Absalon (1128 - 21. marts 1201) var en politisk indflydelsesrig dansk biskop og ærkebiskop. Født i Fjenneslev. Død d. 21. marts 1201 i Sorø Kloster og begravet i Sorø Klosterkirke. Fosterbror til Valdemar 1. den Store og bror til 
2 CONC Esbern Snare. Absalon var af den indflydelsesrige Hvideslægt, hans forældre var Asser Rig og Inger Eriksdatter og hans farfar var Skjalm Hvide.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Liv'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans far Asser Rig var en af rigets mægtigste mænd, og hans mor Inge var datter den svenske konge, Erik den Hellige. Stormandsgården i Fjenneslev på Midtsjælland var Absalons barndomshjem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Allerede fra fødslen var det sikkert meningen, at Absalon som den yngste dreng skulle gøre karriere inden for kirken, medens hans storebror bestyrede familiens ejendomme.
2 CONT
2 CONT Det skulle komme til at få stor betydning at brødrene Absalon og Esbern voksede op sammen med den senere konge, den forældreløse Valdemar, hvis far Knud Lavard var blevet dræbt i 1131. De tre drenge sluttede fostbroderskab sammen.
2 CONC  Fra cirka 1146-1156 var han under uddannelse som teolog på universitetet i Paris, hvor han bl.a. lærte den senere Abbed Vilhelm at kende.
2 CONT
2 CONT Absalon var deltager i Blodgildet i Roskilde, hvor Svend Grathe efter sigende skulle have forsøgt at dræbe Valdemar. Absalon hjalp Valdemar med at flygte til Jylland, hvor Svend Grathe blev besejret. Da Valdemar i 1157 blev konge 
2 CONC over Danmark, fik Absalon stor indflydelse på regeringen
2 CONT
2 CONT Absalon blev biskop i Roskilde i 1158 på foranledning af den nykårede konge Valdemar. I denne egenskab omdannede han benediktinerklosteret i Sorø, som faderen havde grundlagt, til et velstående cistercienserkloster med en klosterk
2 CONC irke, der som den eneste del af klosteret er bevaret intakt, med undtagelse af klosterporten. I Sorø Akademi, der er indrettet i resterne af de tidligere klosterbygninger, findes rester af murværk fra klostertiden. Absalon var en 
2 CONC del af hoffet og som adelsmand en del af den kongelige hærstyrke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han fik i 1160'erne overdraget landsbyen Havn og tillæggende byer som len (der senere blev til København) af kongen, hvor han i 1167 byggede en borg på en holm ude i vandet. Rester af Absalons Borg ligger stadig under Christiansbo
2 CONC rg. Absalon var sammen med Valdemar den Store med til at bekæmpe de vendiske sørøvere, og i 1169 var han med, da vendernes Rügen, dele af Pommern og den vendiske hovedby Arkona blev indtaget.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han blev i 1178 ærkebiskop i Lund efter Ærkebiskop Eskil, og indledte med at virke for, at den kanoniske ret efterlevedes, men kom derfor snart i åben konfrontation med folket i det østensundske Danmark. I 1180-1182 kuede han med 
2 CONC velvillig bistand af de skånelandske stormænd på den mest brutale vis de skånelandske bønders oprør mod hans politik. En politik, der bl.a. gik ud på indførelse af bispetiende. Efter at Knud 6. var blevet konge i 1182, var Absalon
2 CONC  i realiteten den, der styrede Danmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Efter sin død i 1201 blev han som biskop efterfulgt af sin fætter Anders Sunesen. Absalon gravlagt i Sorø klosterkirke, som han selv havde ladet opføre. Liget blev lagt i en blykiste, der nedsattes i en muret grav i højkoret. Absa
2 CONC lons ligsten blev opsat i 1536. Det var flere hundrede år efter hans død, så der er ikke tale om et egentligt portræt.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sagn om Absalons fødsel'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ifølge et gammelt sagn af Saxo blev Absalon født da hans fader Asser Rig var i vendernes land. Asser Rig havde, inden han rejste fra sin gravide kone, sagt at hvis hun således fødte en pige mens han var borte skulle der bygges et 
2 CONC spir på Fjenneslev Kirke, som han var i færd med at bygge, men hvis der blev født en dreng skulle der et rigtigt tårn på kirken. Da Asser Rig senere kom hjem, var der to tårne på Kirken, han havde fået tvillinger (Esbern Snare og 
2 CONC Absalon).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sagn om Absalon i krig'''
2 CONT
2 CONT I 1159 finder vi første gang vidnesbyrd om bispen med sværd i hånd. Palmesøndag var Absalon blevet alarmeret om en vendisk landgang ved Boeslunde, der dengang var vandforbunden med Storebælt; da vinder Absalon navnkundig hæder, da
2 CONC  han med atten af sine hofmænd går de vendiske vikinger i møde, skønt det var mandskabet fra fire og tyve skibe, de fik at kæmpe imod. Sagnet vil vide, at han nedlagde størstedelen, og drev de andre på flugt ind i de nærliggende s
2 CONC kove. Det hedder derfor om ham: „med Peders Sværd den Hyrde god/ ej fared frem med Lempe." Herom vidner en mindesten sat ved Boeslunde Kirke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Om Absalon står der respekt hos Saxo Grammaticus – som biskop og som kæmpe (kriger):
2 CONT
2 CONT Med Bispestaven Kiempen ståer en Konning mellem Klerke
2 CONT
2 CONT Med Ordets Glavind ingen ståer som Absalon hin stærke
2 CONT
2 CONT Med Hellebard og Brynje blå på Hingsten Bispen skinner
2 CONT
2 CONT På blodig Val ej Dansken så en større Sejervinder
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mindesmærker for Absalon'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Højbro Plads:'' 1901, som var 700-året for Absalons død, blev der på Højbro Plads i København indviet en rytterstatue, hvor ærkebiskoppen skuer over mod Slotsholmen, hvor han grundlagde sin borg. Statuen blev udført af billedhug
2 CONC geren Vilhelm Bissen og viser byens grundlægger siddende højt på sin stejlende hest. Han er skildret som kriger i hjelm og ringbrynje og med stridsøksen i hånden. Hans træk menes at være taget fra ligstenen på hans sarkofag i Sorø
2 CONC . Sokkelens udformning skyldes Martin Nyrop, som også formulerede indskriften: "Han var modig, snild og fremsynet - en ynder af lærdom - med ren vilje Danmarks trofaste søn". Den røde teglstenssokkel er dekoreret med vikingeslyng 
2 CONC og svømmende sild som en hetydning til Øresunds skjulte rigdom og indtægtskilde. Desuden ses et våbenskjold med rosenkors og to himmelnøgler på kors, en henvisning til Absalons mere fredelige virke som Roskildebisp.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Københavns Rådhus'': 1901 blev der midt på rådhusets facade mod Rådhuspladsen indviet en portrætstatue delvis forgyldt i bronze af Absalon, også udført af Vilhelm Bissen. Absalon er fremstillet under en baldakin, forgyldt i bisk
2 CONC oppeligt ornat, med bispehue og krumstav. Frisen derover er udført efter tegning af Martin Nyrop. Statuen blev modelleret 1872 og omarbejdet og støbt 1899-1900. På konsollens forside er der indgraveret i bronzen: "S D G" [Solo Dei
2 CONC  Gloria]. I skriftbånd rundt langs nichens kant er der indgraveret: "ERKEBISKOP ABSALON MCXXVIII-MCCI SIÆLLANDS BISKOP DENNE STADS GRVNDLÆGGER"
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Åbningen af Absalons grav'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Liget blev lagt i en blykiste, der nedsattes i en muret grav i højkoret. I 1536 blev graven åbnet for første gang i overværelse af Christian 3, og her fandt man ærkebispen liggende i fuldt skrud med bispehue og bispestav. Ved samm
2 CONC e lejlighed blev der udført en prægtig gravsten i ny renæssance-stil. Kongen tilbød ved den lejlighed ringen til biskop Peder Palladius, der dog afslog. Ringen, der er af guld med en indfældet blå safir blev opdaget, da graven ble
2 CONC v åbnet for anden gang i begyndelsen af 1800-tallet, og er i nutiden udstillet på Nationalmuseet i København. Graven har i øvrigt været åbnet en tredje gang, i 1947.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Gengivet fra wikipedia: http://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absalon)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eide Fjenneslev, Erikslev, Holbækgård, Krænkerup og Læsøgård.  Også Ellingse
2 CONT
2 CONT Biskop i Roskilde i 1158-1191 Ærkebiskop 1178-1201 I 1157 bygget borgen i København
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.denstoredanske.dk/Dansk_Biografisk_Leksikon/Kirke_og_tro/%C3%86rkebiskop/Absalon?highlight=%C3%A6rkebiskop%20absalon
2 CONT
2 CONT http://videnskab.dk/kultur-samfund/kobenhavn-var-en-storby-laenge-absalon
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absalon
2 CONT
2 CONT Absalon (c. 1128 – 21 March 1201) was a Danish statesman and prelate of the Catholic Church who served as the bishop of Roskilde from 1158 to 1192 and archbishop of Lund from 1178 until his death. He was the foremost politician an
2 CONC d church father of Denmark in the second half of the 12th century, and was the closest advisor of King Valdemar I of Denmark. He was a key figure in the Danish policies of territorial expansion in the Baltic Sea, Europeanization i
2 CONC n close relationship with the Holy See, and reform in the relation between the Church and the public. He combined the ideals of Gregorian Reform with loyal support of a strong monarchical power.
2 CONT
2 CONT Absalon was born into the powerful Hvide clan, and owned great land possessions. He endowed several church institutions, most prominently his family's Sorø Abbey. He was granted lands by the crown, and built the first fortificatio
2 CONC n of the city that evolved into modern-day Copenhagen. His titles were passed on to his nephews Anders Sunesen and Peder Sunesen. He died in 1201, and was interred at Sorø Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT Absalon was born around 1167 near Sorø, Zealand.[citation needed] Due to his name being unusual in Denmark, it is speculated that he was baptized on the Danish "Absalon" name day, 30 October. He was the son of Asser Rig, a magnate
2 CONC  of the Hvide clan from Fjenneslev on Zealand, and Inger Eriksdotter. He was also a kinsman of Archbishop Eskil of Lund. He grew up at the castle of his father, and was brought up alongside his older brother Esbern Snare and the y
2 CONC oung prince Valdemar, who later became King Valdemar I of Denmark. During the civil war following the death of Eric III of Denmark in 1146, Absalon travelled abroad to study theology in Paris, while Esbern fought for Valdemar's as
2 CONC cension to the throne. In Paris, he was influenced by the Gregorian Reform ideals of churchly independence from monarchical rule. He also befriended the canon William of Æbelholt at the Abbey of St Genevieve, whom he later made ab
2 CONC bot of Eskilsø Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Absalon first appears in Saxo Grammaticus's contemporary chronicle Gesta Danorum at the end of the civil war, in the brokering of the peace agreement between Sweyn III and Valdemar at St. Alban's Priory in Odense. He was a guest a
2 CONC t the subsequent Roskilde banquet given in 1157 by Sweyn for his rivals Canute V and Valdemar. Both Absalon and Valdemar narrowly escaped assassination by Sweyn on this occasion, and escaped to Jutland, whither Sweyn followed them
2 CONC . Absalon probably did not take part in the following battle of Grathe Heath in 1157, where Sweyn was defeated and slain. This led to Valdemar ascending to the Danish throne. On Good Friday 1158, bishop Asser of Roskilde [Wikidata
2 CONC ] died, and Absalon was eventually elected bishop of Roskilde on Zealand with the help of Valdemar, as the king's reward for Hvide family support.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop and advisor
2 CONT Absalon was a close counsellor of Valdemar, and chief promoter of the Danish crusades against the Wends. Earlier Danish monarchs had failed to subjugate or raid the Pomeranian coasts or make themselves a preeminent power even in t
2 CONC he region of Rana (Rügen), being successfully repelled and raided in turn. During the Danish civil war, Denmark had been open to coastal raids by the ‘Wends’ (which included pagan Rana and the Christian Duchy of Pomerania).
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time, it is worth noting that heading great raids was the Christian Duchy of Pomerania (a tributary of the Duchy of Poland) as well as the) who had not long earlier together seized the capital of Roskilde together with them
2 CONC  and the Pomeranian Duke’s men pillaged the important port of Kungahälla (concurrently with the Wendish Crusade, besides the pillaging goal also preventing Danish participation on the side of the crusaders, which targeted besides 
2 CONC pagan West Slavs also Christian Pomerania and notably besieged the Christian coastal town of Szczecin).
2 CONT
2 CONT The “Wends” had raided the Danish coasts during the civil war of Sweyn III, Canute V, and Valdemar, and at the accession of Valdemar one-third of Denmark lay wasted and depopulated. Absalon’s goal therefore was to increase and cen
2 CONC tralise Danish power in the region, against the coast, deterring raids and seizing from rival influence at least the area of the island of Rana, which was an important West Slavic pagan spot (from which at least some of the raidin
2 CONC g forces originated, and which was under the influence of Christian coastal rivals).
2 CONT
2 CONT Absalon formed a guardian fleet, built coastal defenses, and led several campaigns against the Wends. He even advocated forgiving the earlier enemies of Valdemar, which helped stabilize Denmark internally.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wendish campaigns
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop Absalon topples the god Svantevit at Arkona, as imagined by Laurits Tuxen
2 CONT The first expedition against the Wends conducted by Absalon in person, set out in 1160. These expeditions were successful, but brought no lasting victories. What started out as mere retribution, eventually evolved into full-fledge
2 CONC d campaigns of expansion with religious motives. In 1164 began twenty years of crusades against the Wends, sometimes with the help of German duke Henry the Lion, sometimes in opposition to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1168 the chief Rana fortress at Arkona in Rügen (or Rana in the local language), containing the sanctuary of their god Svantevit, was conquered. The people of Rana agreed to accept Danish suzerainty and the Christian religion a
2 CONC t the same time. From Arkona, Absalon proceeded by sea to Charenza, in the midst of Rügen, the political capital of the Wends and an all but impregnable stronghold. But the unexpected fall of Arkona had terrified the garrison, whi
2 CONC ch surrendered unconditionally at the first appearance of the Danish ships. Absalon, with only Bishop Sweyn of Aarhus and twelve "housecarls", thereupon disembarked, passed between a double row of Wendish warriors, 6000 strong, al
2 CONC ong the narrow path winding among the morasses, to the gates of the fortress, and, proceeding to the temple of the seven-headed god Rugievit, caused the idol to be hewn down, dragged forth and burnt. The whole population of Garz w
2 CONC as then baptized, and Absalon laid the foundations of twelve churches in the isle of Rügen. Rügen was then subjected to Absalon's Bishopric of Roskilde.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Absalon's biggest victories, at Julin.
2 CONT The destruction of this chief sally-port of the Wendish raiders enabled Absalon to considerably reduce the Danish fleet. But he continued to keep a watchful eye over the Baltic, and in 1170 destroyed another pirate stronghold, far
2 CONC ther eastward, at Dziwnów on the isle of Wolin. Absalon's last military exploit came in 1184, off Stralsund at Whitsun, when he soundly defeated a Pomeranian fleet that had attacked Denmark's vassal, Jaromar of Rügen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Policies
2 CONT Absalon's main political goal was to free Denmark from entanglements with the Holy Roman Empire. Absalon reformed the Danish church organisation to closer match Holy See praxis, and worked to keep Denmark a close ally of the Holy 
2 CONC See. However, during the schism between Pope Alexander III and Antipope Victor IV, Absalon stayed loyal to Valdemar even as he joined the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick Barbarossa in supporting Victor IV. This caused a split within 
2 CONC the Danish church, as it possibly forced Eskil of Lund into exile around 1161, despite Absalon's attempts to keep the Danish church united. It was contrary to Absalon's advice and warnings that Valdemar I rendered fealty to the em
2 CONC peror Frederick Barbarossa at Dole in 1162. When Valdemar returned to Denmark, he was convinced to strengthen the Danevirke fortifications at the German border, with the support of Absalon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Absalon built churches and monasteries, supporting international religious orders like the Cistercians and Augustinians, founding schools and doing his utmost to promote civilization and enlightenment. In 1162, Absalon transformed
2 CONC  the Sorø Abbey of his family from Benedictine to Cistercian, granting it lands from his personal holdings. In 1167, Absalon was granted the land around the city of Havn (English: "Harbour"), and built there a castle for coastal d
2 CONC efense against the Wends. Havn quickly expanded into one of Scandinavia's most important centers of trade, and eventually evolved into modern-day Copenhagen. It was also Absalon who held the first Danish Synod at Lund in 1167. He 
2 CONC was interested in history and culture, and commissioned Saxo Grammaticus to write Gesta Danorum, a comprehensive chronicle of the history of the Danes. In 1171, Absalon issued the "Zealand church law" (Danish: Sjællandske Kirkelov
2 CONC ), which reduced the number of Canonical Law offenses for which the church could fine the public, while instituting the tithe payment system. Violation of the law was specified as subject to a secular legal process.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archbishop of Lund
2 CONT Archbishop Eskil returned from exile in 1167. Eskil agreed on canonizing Valdemar's father Knud Lavard in 1170, with Absalon assisting him at the feast. When Eskil stepped down as Archbishop of Lund in 1177, he chose Absalon as hi
2 CONC s successor. Absalon initially resisted the new position, as he did not want to lose his power position on Zealand, but complied with Papal orders to do so in 1178. By a unique Papal dispensation, Absalon was allowed to simultaneo
2 CONC usly maintain his post as Bishop of Roskilde. As the Archbishop of Lund, Absalon utilized ombudsmen from Zealand, demanded unfree labour from the peasantry, and instituted tithes. He was a harsh and effective ruler, who cleared al
2 CONC l Orthodox Christian liturgical remnants in favour of Papal standards. A rebellion in the Scanian peasantry forced him to flee to Zealand in 1180, but he returned and subdued the Scanians with the help of Valdemar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Valdemar died in 1182 and was succeeded by his son, Canute VI, whom Absalon also served as counsellor. Under Canute VI, Absalon was the chief policymaker in Danish politics. Absalon kept his hostile attitude to the Holy Roman Empi
2 CONC re. On the accession of Canute VI in 1182, an imperial ambassador arrived at Roskilde to get the new king to swear fealty to Frederick Barbarossa, but Absalon resolutely withstood him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT When Absalon retired from military service in 1184 at the age of fifty-seven, he resigned the command of fleets and armies to younger men, like Duke Valdemar, the later king Valdemar II. He instead confined himself to the administ
2 CONC ration of the Danish empire. In 1192, Absalon made his nephew Peder Sunesen [da] his successor as Bishop of Roskilde, while his other nephew Anders Sunesen was named the chancellor of Canute VI. Absalon died at Sorø Abbey on 21 Ma
2 CONC rch 1201, 73 years old, with his last will granting his personal holdings to the Abbey, apart from Fjenneslev which went to Esbern Snarre. He had already given Copenhagen to the Bishopric of Roskilde. Absalon was interred at Sorø 
2 CONC Abbey,[5] and was succeeded as Archbishop of Lund by Anders Sunesen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Equestrian statue from 1902 commemorating Absalon on Højbro Plads, Copenhagen
2 CONT Saxo Grammaticus' Gesta Danorum was not finished until after the death of Absalon, but Absalon was one of the chief heroic figures of the chronicle, which was to be the main source of knowledge about early Danish history. Absalon 
2 CONC left a legacy as the foremost politician and churchfather of Denmark in the 12th century. Absalon was equally great as churchman, statesman, and warrior. His policy of expansion was to give Denmark the dominion of the Baltic for t
2 CONC hree generations. That he enjoyed warfare there can be no doubt; yet he was not like the ordinary fighting bishops of the Middle Ages, whose sole indication of their religious role was to avoid the shedding of blood by using a mac
2 CONC e in battle instead of a sword. Absalon never neglected his ecclesiastical duties.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 2000s, "Absalon" was adopted as the name for a class of Royal Danish Navy vessels, and the lead vessel of the class. HDMS Absalon and Esbern Snare were launched and commissioned by Denmark in 2004 and 2005.
