1 NOTE {geni:about_me} = SVERKER II "den yngre/the younger" King of Sweden=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of King Charles VII Sverkersson of Sweden and Queen Christina Hvide 
2 CONT * He succeeded in 1196 as SVERKER II "den yngre/the younger" King of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sverker was a son of King Karl Sverkersson of Sweden and Queen Christine Stigsdatter of Hvide, a Danish noblewoman. Through his mother, he was a cousin's son of the Danish kings Canute VI and Valdemar Sejr. His parents' marriage
2 CONC  has been dated to 1162 or more probably 1163.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SWEDEN.htm#SverkerIIdied1210 KINGS of SWEDEN - FAMILY of SVERKER]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Project MedLands Sweden'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''SVERKER''', son of --- (-murdered 24/25 Dec 1156).  Sverker's parentage is not known.  According to Saxo Grammaticus, he was "of modest origins"[122].  Under King in Östergötland.  He was installed as '''SVERKER I King of Swede
2 CONC n in [1133/34] in succession to Magnus Nielsson of Denmark. '''married firstly as her third husband, ULVHILD Haakonsdotter,''' widow first of INGE II Halstensson King of Sweden and secondly of NIELS King of Denmark, daughter of HA
2 CONC AKON Finsson & his wife --- (-before 1143).'''  Her second marriage is referred to by Saxo Grammaticus who states that "Ulvildam Noricam", wife of "Nicolaus", was secretly abducted by King Sverker but their "connection was accepte
2 CONC d as a marriage"[123].  Fagrskinna names “Úlfhildr dróttning, dóttir Hákonar Finnssunar Hárekssunar or Þjóttu” as mother of “Karl konungs”, adding that she had first married “Nikolás Danakonungr”, secondly “Ingi Sviakonungr Hallst
2 CONC einssunr” and thirdly “Sverkir konungr Kolssunr”[124]. '''married secondly (after 1143) as her third husband, RYKSA Swantosława of Poland,''' widow firstly of MAGNUS I "den Stærke/the Strong" King of Denmark and secondly of [VOLOD
2 CONC AR], daughter of BOLESŁAW III "Krzywousty/Wrymouth" Prince of Poland & his second wife Salome von Berg-Schelklingen ([1116/17]-after 25 Dec 1155).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Rikissam" as the only daughter 
2 CONC of "dux Vergescelaus de Polonia" and his wife Agnes, specifying that "primo fuit regina Suecie", that by her second husband "regi Russie nomine Musuch" she was mother of "Sophiam reginam Dacie et Rikissam", the latter marrying "im
2 CONC peratoris Castelle Alfunso"[125].  This appears to be a confused account which contradicts other sources in many aspects.  She was known as RIKISSA in Sweden.  Her third marriage is confirmed by Knytlíngasaga which records that [h
2 CONC er son] “Knúti konúngi” fled to ”Sörkvir konúngr átti Rikizu, módur Þeirra Knúts konúngs ok Súffiu” after being defeated by King Svend III[126].  The marriage is also confirmed by the Liber Census Daniæ which records that the esta
2 CONC te of [her son by King Sverker] “Bulizlaus” was inherited by his sister Sofia Queen of Denmark [Ryksa’s daughter by her second husband][127]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Sverker I & his first wife ULVHILD Haakonsdotter had four children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1.         '''JOHAN Sverkersson''' (-murdered [1153/54]).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  He appears to have been his father's designated heir but was killed (by peasants?) some yea
2 CONC rs before his father's death[128].   
2 CONT * 2.         '''KARL Sverkersson''' (-murdered Visingsö 12 Apr [1166], bur Alvastra Abbey).  His parentage is stated by Saxo Grammaticus[129].  '''He succeeded in 1161 as KARL I King of Sweden.'''  '''married (1163) KRISTIN Stigsd
2 CONC atter [Hvide], daughter of STIG Tokesen "Hvitaleder/White leather" [Hvide] & his wife Margrete Knudsdatter of Denmark.''' Snorre names (in order) "the Danish king Valdemar…and daughters Margaret, Christina and Catherine" as the ch
2 CONC ildren of "Canute Lavard" & his wife, recording that Margrete married "Stig Hvitaled" and that their daughter was "Christina, married to the Swedish king, Karl Sorkvison, and their son was king Sorkver"[130].  Fagrskinna names (in
2 CONC  order) “Valdimarr konungr ok Kristin ok Katerin or Margareta” as children of “Knútr lávardr, bródir Eiriks eimuna” and his wife, noting that Margrete married “Stigr hvitaledr”, father of “Nikoláss ok Kristinar er átti Karl konung
2 CONC r Sverkissunr”[131].  Morkinskinna records that “Karl Sørkvisson king of the Swedes” married “Kristín” daughter of “Stígr hvítaledr” and his wife Margret[132]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''' King Karl & his wife KRISTIN Stigsdatter  had one child'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>* a)        '''SVERKER Karlson'''(-killed in battle Gestilren 17 Jul 1210, bur Alvastra Abbey).  Snorre names "king Sorkver" as son of "the Swedish king, Karl Sorkvison" & his wife[133].  Fagrskinna names “Sverkir konungr, fadi
2 CONC r Jóans konungs” as son of “Kristinar er átti Karl konungr Sverkissunr”[134].  '''He succeeded in 1196 as SVERKER II "den yngre/the younger" King of Sweden.'''  The Saga of King Sverre records the accession of "Sorkvi Karlsson" af
2 CONC ter the death of "King Knut of Sweden"[135].  “Swerco filius Karoli Regis rex Sweorum” donated property to the monks of Nydala by charter dated to [1196/1210][136].  The Icelandic Annals record the battle in 1208 between "Svercher
2 CONC um Caroli filius" and "Ericum Canuti filium, Suecorum reges"[137].  Deposed 1208.  '''married firstly BENGTE Ebbesdatter Galen,''' daughter of EBBE Sunesen [Galen] from Knardrup & his wife ---.  The primary source which confirms h
2 CONC er parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  '''married secondly INGEGÄRD Birgersdotter, daughter of BIRGER Bengtsson "Brosa" Jarl in Sweden & his wife Brigida of Norway (-after 1210).'''  Snorre names (in order) "Ingeg
2 CONC erd…married to the Swedish king Sorkver [and] a second daughter…Kristin and a third Margaret" as the daughters of "Earl Birger Brose" & his wife[138].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Sverker II & his first wife BENGTE Ebbesdatter  had two children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>* i)          '''KARL Sverkersson''' (-murdered in the mountains near Trondheim 1198).  The Saga of King Sverre records the marriage of "Karlson of King Sorkvi" and "Ingibiorg daughter of King Sverri"[139].  '''married INGEB
2 CONC ORG Sverresdatter,''' of Norway, daughter of SVERRE King of Norway & his first wife Astrid Rösdatter.
2 CONT >>>>>>* ii)         '''HELENA Sverkersdotter''' (-after 1240).  A charter dated 1237 refers to the marriage of “S. Fulconis ducis filius” and “E. Suerchonis Regis filia” after her abduction from Vreta convent[140].  '''married  (b
2 CONC efore 1237) SUNE Folkason Jarl in Sweden, son of FOLKER Birgersson [Folkunge] Jarl in Sweden & his wife (-1247).'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Sverker II & his second wife INGEGÄRD Birgersdotter had two children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>* iii)        '''KARL Sverkersson''' (-1213).  The Icelandic Annals record the death in 1213 of "Carolus Svercheri filius"[141].  If King Sverker was his father, Karl must have been from the king´s second marriage, after the
2 CONC  death of his older half-brother of the same name.] 
2 CONT >>>>>>* iv)       '''JOHAN Sverkersson''' (1201-Visingsö 10 Mar 1222, bur Alvastra Abbey).  Snorre names "King Jon" as the son of "the Swedish king Sorkver" and his wife Ingegerd[142].  Morkinskinna names “King Jón” as son of “Kin
2 CONC g Sørkvir”[143].  The Saga of King Sverre records the death of "Earl Birgi Brosa" in the same year as Sverre King of Norway [in 1202], commenting that "the Swedes then took Jon son of King Sorkvi…one year old"[144].  Fagrskinna na
2 CONC mes “Sverkir konungr, fadir Jóans konungs” as son of “Kristinar er átti Karl konungr Sverkissunr”[145].  '''He succeeded in 1216 as JOHAN I King of Sweden, crowned [1219].'''  The Icelandic Annals record the succession in 1216 of 
2 CONC "Johannes Svercheri filius" who reigned for six years[146].  The Icelandic Annals record the death in 1222 of "Johannes Sverkeri films rex Suecorum"[147]. 
2 CONT * 3.         '''INGEGÄRD''' (-1172, bur Vreta Abbey).  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  '''married  (1156) KNUD III Magnussen,''' Joint King of Denmark, son of MAGNUS I "de
2 CONC n Stærke/the Strong" King of Denmark & his wife Ryksa [Swantosława] of Poland ([1129]-murdered Roskilde 9 Aug 1157).
2 CONT * 4.         '''INGEGÄRD''' (-1204).  The primary source which confirms her parentage has not yet been identified.  Prioress at Vretakloster 1164.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Sverker I & his second wife RYKSA Swantosława of Poland had one child'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 5.         '''BURISLAV''' (-before 1173).  His parentage is indicated by the following document: the Liber Census Daniæ records that the estate of “Bulizlaus”, son of King Sverker, was inherited by his [half-]sister Sofia Queen 
2 CONC of Denmark[148].    Contender for the throne [1168/73]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Sverker had one [illegitimate] son by an unknown mistress'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 6.          '''KOL''' The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =History=
2 CONT
2 CONT Sverker was a son of King Karl Sverkersson of Sweden and Queen Christine Stigsdatter of Hvide[3], a Danish noblewoman. His parents' marriage has been dated to 1162 or 1163.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father Karl had been murdered in Visingsö in 1167, apparently by minions of the next king Canute I of Sweden, Sverker was taken to Denmark while a boy and grew up with his mother's clan of Hvide, leaders of Zealand. Sverk
2 CONC er also allied himself with the Galen clan leaders in Skåne who were close to the Hvide, by marriage through lady Benedikte Ebbesdotter of Hvide. The Danish king supported him as claimant to Sweden, thus helping to destabilize the
2 CONC  neighboring country.
2 CONT
2 CONT When King Canute I of Sweden died in 1196, his sons were only children. Sverker was chosen as the next king of Sweden, surprisingly without quarrel. He returned to his native country, however being regarded quite Danished. His unc
2 CONC ontested election was largely thanks to Jarl Birger Brosa whose daughter, Ingegerd Birgersdotter of Bjelbo, Sverker married soon after his first wife had died.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Reign'''
2 CONT
2 CONT King Sverker confirmed and enlarged privileges for the Swedish church and the Valerius Archbishop of Uppsala. The privilege document of 1200 is the oldest known ecclesiastical privilege in Sweden. Skáldatal names two of Sverker's 
2 CONC court skalds: Sumarliði skáld and Þorgeirr Danaskáld. In 1202 Earl Birger died and the late jarl's grandson, Sverker's one-year old son John received the title of Jarl from his father. This was intended to strengthen him as heir o
2 CONC f the crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1203, Canute's four sons, who had lived in Swedish royal court, began to claim the throne and Sverker exiled them to Norway. His position as king became unsecured from this point forward. The sons of Canute returned with tr
2 CONC oops in 1205, supported by the Norwegian party of Birkebeiner, but Sverker succeeded in winning in the Battle of Älgarås, where three of the sons fell. The only survivor returned with Norwegian support in 1208 and in the Battle of
2 CONC  Lena, Sverker was defeated. Sverker's troops were commanded by Ebbe Suneson, the father of his late first wife and brother of Andreas Sunesen, Archbishop of Lund. King Eric X of Sweden drove Sverker to exile to Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pope Innocentius III's attempt to have the crown returned to Sverker did not succeed. Sverker made a military expedition, with Danish support, to Sweden, but was conquered and killed in the Battle of Gestilren in 1210. The ancient
2 CONC  sources state that "he was killed by the Folkung clan".
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT With his first wife, the Danish noble Benedicta Ebbesdatter (Galen, apparently not Hvide as otherwise alleged, b. c. 1165/70, d. 1200), whom he married before 1190 when yet living in Denmark, Sverker had at least one well-attested
2 CONC  daughter, Helena Sverkersdotter , as well as possibly further children, such as Karl (who died in adolescence at the latest, if ever lived; but his existence is from the record that he is alleged to have married a daughter of kin
2 CONC g Sverre of Norway), and possibly even two other daughters (if they existed, their names are given by reconstructive history research as Margaret and Kristina - however they may just have been Sverker's first wife's kinswomen). La
2 CONC ter pretensions of the House of Mecklenburg claim that Sverker's daughter (if he had such) Christina was their ancestress, wife of Henry II of Mecklenburg ("Henry Borwin" in some later texts).
2 CONT
2 CONT The second marriage in 1200 with Ingegerd Birgersdotter of Bjelbo, daughter of the Folkunge Jarl Birger Brosa produced a son and heir, Jon (1201–1222), who was chosen king of Sweden 1216 as John I of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT His certain daughter Helena Sverkersdotter married (earl) Sune Folkason of the family of Bjelbo, justiciar of Västergötland. Their daughters Catherine of Ymseborg and Benedicta of Ymseborg became pawns in marriages to gain Swedish
2 CONC  succession after 1222, when the Sverker dynasty went extinct in male line. Catherine was married to the rival dynasty's heir Eric XI of Sweden but they remained apparently childless. Benedikte married Svantepolk of Viby and had s
2 CONC everal daughters, who married Swedish noblemen. Several Swedish noble families claim descent from Benedikte.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Links=
2 CONT
