1 OCCU Norges Konge (1204-1217), King of Norway, Konge av Norge 1204 -1217, Konge 1204-1217. Rein Rissa, Konge, Kong Inge av Norge., King of Norway 1204-1217, kpnge, King, Norges konge 1204-1217, Konge av Norge
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Inge II Bårdsson (født 1185, død 1217) var en norsk konge. Han var sønn av Sverre Sigurdssons søster Cecilia og høyadelsmannen Bård Guttormsson av Reinsætten. Hans halvbror var Skule Bårdsson som ble Norges første 
2 CONC hertug. Inge ble valgt til konge av birkebeinerne i 1204. Han hadde kongesete i Trondheim, og hadde også herredømme over store deler av vestkysten. Borgerkrigsperioden i norsk historie varte fra 1130 til 1240. Han kjempet mot bagl
2 CONC erne frem til 1208, da det kom til forlik mellom ham og baglerkongen Filippus Simonsson, som fikk styre over Viken. Forliket fant sted på Kvitsøy i Rogaland og ble kjent som «Kvitsøyforliket». Konfliktene mellom baglere og birkebe
2 CONC inere tok slutt da både Inge og Filippus døde i 1217. Etterfølgeren, Håkon IV Håkonsson, ble anerkjent av både baglere og birkebeinere. Inge er gravlagt i Nidarosdomen i Trondheim.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inge_B%C3%A5rdsson
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inge_II_of_Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Inge Baardson was king of Norway from 1204 to 1217. His reign was within the later stages of the period known in Norwegian history as the age of civil wars. Inge was the king of the birkebeiner faction. The conclusion of the settl
2 CONC ement of Kvitsøy with the bagler faction in 1208 led to peace for the last nine years of Inge’s reigns, at the price of Inge and the birkebeiner recognising bagler rule over Viken (the Oslofjord-area).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Fra Wikipedia, den frie encyklopedi
2 CONT
2 CONT Inge II Bårdsson (født 1185, død 1217) var en norsk konge. Han var sønn av Sverre Sigurdssons søster Cecilia og høyadelsmannen Bård Guttormsson av Reinsætten. Hans halvbror var Skule Bårdsson som ble Norges første hertug. Inge ble
2 CONC  valgt til konge av birkebeinerne i 1204.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han hadde kongesete i Trondheim, og hadde også herredømme over store deler av vestkysten. Borgerkrigsperioden i norsk historie varte fra 1130 til 1240.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han kjempet mot baglerne frem til 1208, da det kom til forlik mellom ham og baglerkongen Filippus Simonsson, som fikk styre over Viken. Forliket fant sted på Kvitsøy i Rogaland og ble kjent som «Kvitsøyforliket». Konfliktene mello
2 CONC m baglere og birkebeinere tok slutt da både Inge og Filippus døde i 1217. Etterfølgeren, Håkon IV Håkonsson, ble anerkjent av både baglere og birkebeinere. Inge er gravlagt i Nidarosdomen i Trondheim.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT     * OCCUPATION: Norges konge 1204-1217
2 CONT     * BIRTH: 1185, Rein, Rissa
2 CONT     * DEATH: 23 Apr 1217 
2 CONT
2 CONT Konge av Norge 1204 - 1217
2 CONT
2 CONT Inge II Baardsson , King of Norway (1)  M, #113015, b. 1185, d. 1217 Last Edited=10 May 2003 Consanguinity Index=0.15%
2 CONT      Inge II Baardsson , King of Norway was born in 1185. (1) He was the son of Baard Guttormsson and Cecily Sigurdsdottir. (1) He married Cyrid (?). (1)  He died in 1217. (1)
2 CONT      Inge II Baardsson , King of Norway succeeded to the title of King Inge II of Norway in 1205. (1) Child of Inge II Baardsson , King of Norway and Cyrid (?) -1. Guttorm Ingesson d. c 1230 (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Inge Baardson (1185 - 23 April 1217), (Norwegian Inge Bårdsson, Old Norse Ingi Bárðarson), was king of Norway from 1204 to 1217. His reign was within the later stages of the period known in Norwegian history as the age of civil wa
2 CONC rs. Inge was the king of the birkebeiner faction. The conclusion of the settlement of Kvitsøy with the bagler faction in 1208 led to peace for the last nine years of Inge’s reigns, at the price of Inge and the birkebeiner recognis
2 CONC ing bagler rule over Viken (the Oslofjord-area).
2 CONT
2 CONT Inge’s father, Bård, was a prominent lendmann from the Trøndelag region. He was an early supporter of king Sverre, who brought the birkebeiner faction to power in the late 12th century after years of war against king Magnus Erling
2 CONC sson. Inge’s mother, Cecilia, was the daughter of an earlier king, Sigurd Munn. She had been married to the lawspeaker Folkvid in Värmland in Sweden. After her brother, Sverre, had won the throne of Norway, she left her husband an
2 CONC d travelled to Sverre in Norway, claiming she had been wedded to Folkvid against her will. The archbishop annulled her marriage to Folkvid, and Sverre gave her to his trusted follower Bård Guttormsson in marriage. Inge was Bård an
2 CONC d Cecilia’s only son. [edit] Accession
2 CONT
2 CONT After king Sverre died in 1202, his son, Haakon, and his grandson, Guttorm, died within two years. The birkebeiner were thus left without any direct successors of Sverre to choose as their king (the existence of another grandson o
2 CONC f Sverre, Haakon Haakonsson, was as yet unknown to them). Sverre’s old adversaries, the bagler, were exploiting the situation to launch a new invasion of Viken under their king, Erling Steinvegg. After the infant king Guttorm’s de
2 CONC ath in august 1204, the birkebeiner needed a strong leader to oppose the bagler threat. The birkebeiner leaders wanted earl Haakon the Crazy, who had earlier been appointed to rule the kingdom in king Guttorm’s infancy. Haakon was
2 CONC  Inge’s older half-brother, the son of Cecilia and Folkvid. However, Eirik, archbishop of Nidaros, and the farmers of Trøndelag insisted on choosing Inge, who had until then ruled Trøndelag under Guttorm. A compromise was reached,
2 CONC  whereby Inge became king, while earl Haakon became leader of the army, and received half the royal income. [edit] Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT The next four years saw intense fighting between the birkebeiner and the bagler. The bagler king Erling died in 1206, but the bagler continued the fight under their new king, Philippus Simonsson. The bagler mostly controlled the V
2 CONC iken area, with the cities of Tønsberg and Oslo, king Inge controlled Trøndelag with Nidaros, while Bergen in western Norway changed hands several times. On 22 April 1206 the bagler attacked Nidaros during wedding celebrations for
2 CONC  Inge’s sister Sigrid, and Inge himself only barely escaped with his life after swimming the Nidelva river in freezing temperatures. The next year, the birkebeiner launched a successful attack on the bagler stronghold of Tønsberg,
2 CONC  but the war dragged on with neither side able to gain a decisive victory. In autumn of 1207, archbishop Tore of Nidaros and bishop Nikolas of Oslo, a prominent bagler, started negotiations for a settlement of the birkebeiner and 
2 CONC bagler. They succeeded in bringing about a meeting between the kings Inge and Philippus, and earl Haakon, at Kvitsøy in Rogaland in the autumn of 1208. A settlement was made, whereby Philippus agreed to give up the title of king a
2 CONC nd his royal seal. He was to remain in control of eastern Norway with Viken, except Bohuslän, with the title of earl under king Inge. Earl Haakon was given western Norway with Bergen, while Inge would be the only king, overlord of
2 CONC  Philippus and Haakon and direct ruler of Trøndelag with Nidaros. To seal the treaty, Philippus married king Sverre’s daughter and king Inge’s cousin, Kristina Sverresdotter.
2 CONT
2 CONT The peace treaty held for the rest of Inge’s reign. However, Philippus did not respect its provisions and continued to use the title of king, and kept his royal seal. The relationship between Inge and his brother Haakon seems to h
2 CONC ave been at times tense. When it became clear that Philippus was continuing to call himself king, Haakon made attempts to have himself declared king as well, but Inge refused to accept this. Instead, an agreement was drawn up by w
2 CONC hich the brother that survived the other would inherit the other’s lands, while a legitimate son of either would inherit them both. Haakon had a legitimate son, while Inge only had an illegitimate son, Guttorm (b. 1206) by a concu
2 CONC bine called Gyrid. In 1214, Inge suppressed a rising by the farmers of Trøndelag – earl Haakon was suspected of having had a hand in the rising. Open conflict between the two brothers never broke out, however, and Haakon died of n
2 CONC atural causes in Bergen just after christmas of 1214. Inge took over his part of the kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1217, Inge fell ill in Nidaros. During his illness, he appointed his younger half-brother, Skule Bårdsson, earl and leader of the army. On 23 April 1217, Inge died. He was buried in Nidaros Cathedral. He was succeeded as king b
2 CONC y the 13-year-old Haakon Haakonsson, an illegitimate grandson of king Sverre, who had been raised at the courts of king Inge and earl Haakon since they became aware of his existence in 1206. Skule continued as earl and de facto ru
2 CONC ler for the next few years. [edit] Appraisal of Inge
2 CONT
2 CONT Many historians have seen Inge as a rather weak king. He never achieved control over all of Norway, and agreed to a power-sharing with the bagler, which he stuck to even though Philippus broke the agreement by continuing to style 
2 CONC himself as "king". As a reaction to such views, others have claimed that Inge was a strong ruler, in that he was able to withstand the pressure of the more war-like among the birkebeiner and put a halt to the destructive civil war
2 CONC s for a time.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bagler sagas - a contemporary source - describes Inge as a quiet and calm man, who shied away from feasting and preferred to spend his time in his own quarters with close friends - a character trait which was held against him 
2 CONC by some of his men. He was weak of health after his near brush with death during the attack on Nidaros in 1206.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Inge’s father, Bård, was a prominent lendmann from the Trøndelag region. He was an early supporter of king Sverre, who brought the birkebeiner faction to power in the late 12th century after years of war against king Magnus Erling
2 CONC sson. Inge’s mother, Cecilia, was the daughter of an earlier king, Sigurd Munn. She had been married to the lawspeaker Folkvid in Värmland in Sweden. After her brother, Sverre, had won the throne of Norway, she left her husband an
2 CONC d travelled to Sverre in Norway, claiming she had been wedded to Folkvid against her will. The archbishop annulled her marriage to Folkvid, and Sverre gave her to his trusted follower Bård Guttormsson in marriage. Inge was Bård an
2 CONC d Cecilia’s only son.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Inge II Bårdsson (født 1185, død 1217) var en norsk konge. Han var sønn av Sverre Sigurdssons søster Cecilia og høyadelsmannen Bård Guttormsson av Reinsætten. Hans halvbror var Skule Bårdsson som ble Norges første hertug. Inge ble
2 CONC  valgt til konge av birkebeinerne i 1204.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han hadde kongesete i Trondheim, og hadde også herredømme over store deler av vestkysten. Borgerkrigsperioden i norsk historie varte fra 1130 til 1240.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han kjempet mot baglerne frem til 1208, da det kom til forlik mellom ham og baglerkongen Filippus Simonsson, som fikk styre over Viken. Forliket fant sted på Kvitsøy i Rogaland og ble kjent som «Kvitsøyforliket». Konfliktene mello
2 CONC m baglere og birkebeinere tok slutt da både Inge og Filippus døde i 1217. Etterfølgeren, Håkon IV Håkonsson, ble anerkjent av både baglere og birkebeinere. Inge er gravlagt i Nidarosdomen i Trondheim.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Inge II Bårdsson (født 1185, død 1217) var en norsk konge. Han var sønn av Sverre Sigurdssons søster Cecilia og høyadelsmannen Bård Guttormsson av Reinsætten. 
2 CONT
