1 NAME Jens Madsen /til Visborg/
2 GIVN Jens Madsen
2 SURN til Visborg
2 _MARNM Munk
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Mariager Kirke
2 ADDR
3 STAE Randers
3 CTRY Danmark
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1415
2 PLAC Visborg, Denmark
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1501
2 ADDR
3 CITY Mariager
3 STAE North Denmark Region
3 CTRY Denmark
1 OCCU Ridder
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Jens Madsen Munk, til Visborg=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Født''':	ca. 1415 
2 CONT * '''Død''':	Før 02 Aug 1501  ''''''Gravlagt''' :	'''Mariager Kirke (Onsild H., Randers)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notater ==
2 CONT * Til Visborg, Kollerup, Rugaard (Sønder H.) og Havnø (Hindsted H.)
2 CONT * var alt 1445 Ridder, beseglede 1455 til Vitterlighed med Niels Munk (Juul) af Ballebo og med Hr. Niels Munk af Brusgaard
2 CONT * udstedte 1460 Gjenbrev til Biskop Knud i Viborg paa 2 Gaarde i Havbro, paa hvilke Bispen havde givet ham og hans Hustru Anne Jensdatter Livsbrev
2 CONT * var s. A. sammen med Hr. Niels Christiernsen (Munk) af Kjølby Voldgiftsmand i Striden mellem Lage Brok og de Rosenkrantz'er
2 CONT * var i det Hele taget en anset Mand, hvis Navn ofte træffes i Datidens Aktstykker
2 CONT * indværgede 1468 med Lovhævd Visborg og Havnø og 1481 Kollerup
2 CONT * arvede 1484 Toftum Gaard og Mølle (Refs H.) for en Broderlod i Arven efter Mogens Nist
2 CONT * var 1487 nærværende ved Hyldingen i Lund
2 CONT * gav 1495 med sin Hustru Gods til et af ham stiftet Kapel i Mariager Kirke
2 CONT * gjorde 1498 Lovhævd paa Gods, som hans Farfader Jens Nielsen og Fader Mads Jensen havde ejet ulast og ukjært
2 CONT * levede 6 April 1501, men var død 2 Aug. s. A., da Skifte holdtes efter ham.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://runeberg.org/trap/3-4/0520.html Kongeriget Danmark / 3. Udgave 4. Bind : Hjørring, Thisted, Aalborg, Viborg og Randers Amter]: Visborggaard (gml. Form: Wæsæburgh) tilhørte 1351 Anders Nielsen (Munk), 1401 dennes Broder Jen
2 CONC s Nielsen, 1408 og 1422 disses Sønner Niels Andersen og Mads Jensen, 1445 og endnu 1495 sidstnævntes Son Hr. Jens Madsen, hans Søn Munk Jensen 1495; da hans Sønner Jens og Jakob Munk døde uden Børn, kom V. til deres Moder Fru Inge
2 CONC r Bjørn, der havde ægtet Jens Tygesen (Seefeld), og  disses Søn Enevold S., † 1557 
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.helberskov.eu/ny_side_59.htm Havnø gods historie]: Gennem hele sidste halvdel af 1400-tallet ejedes Visborggård af Jens Madsen Munk, ridder og en ret betydelig herremand, han indvordede 1468 Visborggårds og Havnøs enem
2 CONC ærker. Allerede inden sin død, der indtraf 1501, havde han delt sit gods mellem sine børn. Sønnen Niels Jensen fik Visborggård, datteren Eline, der var gift med Hans Lykke, fik Havnø, som han skrev sig til; allerede 1502 og 1503 o
2 CONC mtales han i dokumenter son Hans Lykke ”i Havnø”; han har da vistnok på denne tid opført sig en borg på det sted, hvor det nuværende Havnø ligger, og omgivet den med volde og grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.protokoller.dk/diplomatariumhornumnse/Diplomatarium%20Hornumense%20del%201.pdf Diplomatarium Hornumense]; 12. juni 1542 Herredagsdom. Morten Roed mod Malte Lauridsen (Viffert) angaaende en gaard i Guldbæk. Morten Roed 
2 CONC fremlægger et skiftebrev af 1492 lydende, at Jens Madsen (Munk) af Visborg udlagde Fru Ingeborg en gaard i Guldbæk for den del, hun havde i Visborg. Malte Lauridsen hævder, at hans forældre har haft gaarden i mere end 50 aar og fr
2 CONC emlægger en lovhævd af 1480, en dom af kongen, der stadfæstede lovhævden og et skiftebrev, hvori gaarden var tillagt ham i søskendeskifte, og han tildømmesgaarden i henhold til hans adkomstbreve. Der tages ikke hensyn til Morten R
2 CONC oeds skiftebrev, da det ikke bevises, at han og hans arvelader nogensinde har haft gaarden i besiddelse, men der indrømmes ham adgang til evt. at sagsøge arvingerne efter Jens Madsen Munk, død 1501, fa hvem har han sin paastaaede 
2 CONC adkomst. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Morten Roeds broder, Tord Roed, var gift med Anne Andersdatter Friis  af Haraldskær, der første gang var gift med Jens Munk af Visborg, død 1512, og som var sønnesøn af Jens Madsen Munk af Visborg der døde 1501. Det kan være herfr
2 CONC a den paastaaede adkomst stammer.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Kilder==
2 CONT # Danmarks Adels Aarbog, Thiset, Hiort-Lorenzen, Bobé, Teisen., (Dansk Adelsforening), [1884 - 2005]., DAA 1905:301.
2 CONT # http://finnholbek.dk/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I17981&tree=2
2 CONT
2 CONT Madsen Munk
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref:	 	Sources: DA1905-VFr-Roh-
2 CONT Profession:	 	Knight
2 CONT Sheep:	 	Mads Jensen Munk ((13--) - )
2 CONT Mother:	 	Karen Jensdatter Udsen (1400 - )
2 CONT
2 CONT Lived:	 	Visborggård, Visborg parish, Ålborg county
2 CONT Death:	 	1501
2 CONT
2 CONT In the family of Anne Jensdatter Seefeld
2 CONT Wedding:	 	before 1461
2 CONT Children:		Maren Jensdatter Munk (1500 - )
2 CONT Julien Jensdatter Munk (- 1502)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT Sir. Jens Madsen to Visborg, Kollerup, Rugaard (Sønder Herred) and Havnø (Hindsted Herred), was a total of 1445 knights, sealed 1455 to be true with Niels Munk (Juul) of Ballebo and with Hr. Niels Munk af Brusgaard, issued in 1460
2 CONC  a return letter to Bishop Knud in Viborg on 2 farms in Havbro, on which the bishop had given him and his wife Anne Jensdatter a life certificate, was the same year together with Hr. Niels Christiernsen (Monk) of Kjølby, arbitrato
2 CONC r in the dispute between Lage Brok and the Rosenkrantz's, was generally a respected man, whose name is often found in documents of the time, fortified in 1468 with legal claim Visborg and Havnø and 1481 Kollerup, inherited 1484 To
2 CONC ftum Gaard og Mølle (Refs Herred) for a brother's lot in the inheritance after Mogens Nist, was present at the tribute in Lund in 1487, in 1495 gave property with his wife to a chapel he had founded in Mariager Kirke, in 1498 made
2 CONC  a legal claim to property, as his grandfather Jens Nielsen and father Mads Jensen had owned free and unloved, lived 6 April 1501, but had died 2 August the same year, when probate was held after him, buried in Mariager Church; ma
2 CONC rried before 1460 to Anne Jensdatter (Bagge or Seefeld). (DA1906-).
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT 1. Munk Jensen, should actually have been called Niels Jensen, but is said in the family books to have called himself Munk after his mother's maternal uncle Mr. Niels Munk to Brusgaard, to Visborg, in 1487 with his father was co-i
2 CONC ssuer of records of Viborg Parish Council and Hindsted Herredsting, met in 1492 on transfer in Aarhus after his wife's parents, lived 1496, must have led the Danes' main banner in the battle of Brunkebjerg 1497 and be drowned hims
2 CONC elf fourteen, all knights men, on the return journey, buried in Viborg Cathedral; married before 1492 to Inger Andersdatter (Bjørn), who in 1501 met on probate with her siblings after their sister's daughter maiden Anne Axelsdatte
2 CONC r (Thott) and also held probate with her departed husband's sisters after their father Hr. Jens Madsen and mother Mrs. Anne Jensdatter. (She was married the second time to Jens Tygesen Seefeld to Dalsgaard). 
2 CONT 2. Elne to Rugaard and Havnø, living widow 1511; married before 1486 to Hans Lykke til Fannerup. 
2 CONT 3. Benedicte to Kollerup, lived 1511; married to Niels Christensen (Tornekrands) to Kyø, died 1500 (married 1st time to Margrethe Juel). 
2 CONT 4. Marine, lived 1501; married to Jens Pedersen (Vognsen) to Boller. 
2 CONT 5. Margrethe, lived in 1501 and is called Frue. 
2 CONT 6. Julien to Kollerup and Rugård, >tip13, 127875. 
2 CONT 7. Margrethe, mentioned in 1501 and also called Frue.
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughter (no. 4) Marine (Maren) was married a second time to Jesper Lunov. (DA1911R-).
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughter (no. 6) Julien's husband Las Lunov died childless about 1500. (TrRa-).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir. Jens Madsen, who owned Visborg 1452-87, and who already in a court witness dat. 17/2 1452 is mentioned as Ies Mathsen, knight. As he lived nearly half a hundred years after that time, his lordship must then have been of fairl
2 CONC y recent date. - He is often mentioned as a witness in various legal proceedings from that time, just as he is also more often seen to have been an arbitrator, commissioner, etc. - The following is stated about him: In 1460 he dee
2 CONC ded some property in Saltum Parish to Økloster. - In 1466 he obtained a lien on his estate in Als and Buddum (Als Parish). - Two years later, he also got a lien on Visborg and Havnø, and regarding the latter it is stated in the co
2 CONC py of the court witness: 
2 CONT Item he became a Haunøe (with lien) with all his right(s), (so) which are Ager and Meadow(, Forest and Field), Fishing Water, Fægang nothing except(, whatever is or can be mentioned), Ålegårdstader, a Trout Farm in Wedel), and esp
2 CONC ecially a Tagmark for free Enemærke. 
2 CONT In 1470, he recorded a court witness at Moubjergting for information about Skjel at the forest belonging to Als and Buddum. - A Diploma from 1471 reads that Jens Matisson, Knight, in Wesborgh, Morten Swan, Armourer, and 4 farmers 
2 CONC inform King Kristiern that, according to a change of heart desired by Bishop Knudh (in Viborg), they have investigated a Farm in Solbergh and a Cemetery in Lywngby in Hellum Herred and found them equally good. - In 1480, he confes
2 CONC ses that he owes Økloster 40 Rinke guilders, for which he pledges some property.
2 CONT Of course, during his long tenure, he has had many property disputes, some of which can be mentioned here: In 1462, he thus had disputes with Mr. Anders Jakobsen on Stenalt about a meadow, lying in Borup Mark (Asdrup S.) and in Br
2 CONC øndbjerg Mark (Rostrup S.)-. - In 1464 there was a quarrel between him and the Bishop in Viborg "about thre kircker hoesz Wisborg", which Jens Matzen wanted to keep. - In 1476 he had tired of St. Karen's guild in Ålborg for land a
2 CONC nd land, which the guild had as a mortgage, but in the settlement of the same year, he and his heirs got the disputed property in perpetuity. - At the same time, he had a number of disputes with Bishop Knud in Visborg regarding th
2 CONC e fishery in Låstrup. A County Council judgment from 1478 states that Mr. Jens Madsen himself confessed that he did not make the Low claim against Bishop Knud on "lidell Wedell" (a brook near Låstrupå). - In 1482, a court witness 
2 CONC was recorded that the fish farm in lidell Wedell, which Mr. Jens Madsen with Vælde and Uret had had it cut down, had belonged to the Bishopric for 100 years. - These disputes about the right to fish in the streams at Låstrup seem 
2 CONC to have been difficult to settle, but they were very old rights; because already Jens Madsen's great-grandfather is written to Låstrup, and his name also appears in connection with the fishery in lidel Wedel. In a defective record
2 CONC  from 1392 it is stated as follows: "... ffrue Kirstine Niels Sebbesens efftherleuerske hennes bornn och brodher .... af Viboriig for ...n fisherij y Son [der aa och y] lidell Wedell". - Regarding Haubroholm in Hollumby, Farsø Par
2 CONC ish, Jens Madsen also had a number of disputes with Mrs. Kloster in Ålborg. 
2 CONT Since Mads Jensen is not known to have had any other children than the son mentioned here, and since the other members of this line of the family seem to have died out earlier, all the estate was now gathered in one hand. Sir. Jøn
2 CONC s Mattesen af ​​Wiborgh, knight, was thus a great landowner, and in addition to these properties also had Kollerupgård (Hadbjerg S.) and several more estates far and wide in northern Jutland. That he owned Kollerup is evident from
2 CONC  a court witness from Omtr. 1545, whereby Erik Nielsen (Tornekrands) tells Kollerupgård how (his maternal grandfather) Hr. Jens Madsen, Knight, with Lavhævd incorporated Kollerupgård, which he proved with Lavhævd published in 1481
2 CONC . Jens Madsen has therefore inherited this farm through his mother's family, and this also agrees with the fact that Sorte Jens' descendants died out in the second generation. His only son, Lave Jensen, died "old and unmarried", a
2 CONC nd Sorte Jens' only daughter, Else Jensdatter, who was married to "long" Mr. Ove Ovesen of the Mur-Kås clan, left only one daughter, who died unmarried.
2 CONT Sometime before 1490, Knight Jens left Visborggård to his son, Munk Jensen, whereas he himself took up residence at Mariager, where he owned some property. He seems to have left his other properties to some of his sons-in-law at t
2 CONC he same time, among these properties Havnø may have been, but it is not certain that it was separated from Visborg at that time, but it may have only happened with the transfer in 1501. 
2 CONT The 20/12 1498 was awarded Mr. Jens Madsen at Hindsted Herredsting, according to Law claim of 22/5 1466, a part of the estate in Als - among them a Forstrand and Saltkjedel are mentioned, - Helberskov, Buddum and Skjellum, Part in
2 CONC  Louenkjær etc. Part of this estate was acquired by Jens Nielsen of Visborg and had since unlovingly followed his son Matis Jensen and the grandson Mr. Jens Madsen of Visborg. - He took lien on his property by marriage, but later 
2 CONC had a dispute about it, as can be seen from a Viborg county court judgment, dat. 16/2 1499, about a case between well-born man Hans Lykke on Mr. Jens Madsen, Ridders, Vegne, against regular Man, Brother Oluf i Mariager and Nis Mik
2 CONC kelsen. The former claimed that the counterparty had entered into a lawsuit with Mariager Kloster Hr. Jens Madsen's Land and Forest at Mariager, on which he had lien and mortgage deeds. Brother Oluf disputed this, but did not want
2 CONC  to present his letter of claim as it was not yet sealed. It was then said before the Court that it was found that Brother Oluf and Nis Mikkelsen had internalized Mr. Jens Madsen's estate to Mariager Kloster, then their liege shou
2 CONC ld not have power. - On 20/6 1499, Mr. Jens Madsen, Knight's Order Witness at Hindsted Herredsting, that Sandemænd made shields between the Farmer and the Godhouse and correspond to Mariager Kloster every 6th Grass over all Buddum
2 CONC  Field and Forest. 
2 CONT In 1495, he had donated a part of his estate at Mariager to Mariager Kloster, but in such a way that it would only come into possession after his and his wife's death. 
2 CONT He died in 1501 and his wife - who must have died shortly before him - is also buried in Mariager Church, where he had a chapel built. On their still existing tombstone it says - or rather has written: 
2 CONT Hic jacet dns (dominus) Johes (Johannes) 
2 CONT Mathie de Visborh miles com uxore sva dna 
2 CONT Anna Orate procis. (VFr-).
2 CONT
2 CONT About Jens Madsen Munk's daughter, Julien (Jøllin) Jensdatter: 
2 CONT Regarding (...) Jøllin Jensdatter, it can be noted that in her name we have an insignificant clue, which also points in the direction of her mother being a Seefeld, because this The name is so rare that one must have great difficu
2 CONC lty in finding it anywhere; therefore, when we come across this name again in a daughter of Jens Thamesen (Seefeld), this is very telling, and there is no question that, if you could go further back in time, you would come across 
2 CONC this name again in the same family. (...). (VFr-).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
