0 @N6000000000564690337@ NOTE Occupation: Land-owner (Propriet?r)
1 CONT The respected Bornholm historian Dr. M.K. Zarthmann (published in 
1 CONT 1931) relates that Otte Pedersen Uf can be found mentioned in records 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT dating from 1493 and 1497 in which he is mentioned as the owner of 
1 CONT Skovsholm in Ibsker parish; and that it is thought that his wife was 
1 CONT a member of the Galen family. They are attributed as having had at 
1 CONT least four children:
1 CONT 1) Oluf Ottesen, Chief Justice (Landsdommer) of Bornholm from 1508 to 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT 1522;
1 CONT 2) Peder Uf, "Kannik"(?) in Sk¯ne, 1505;
1 CONT 3) Anders Uf, mentioned as "fr?nde" to Anders Galen in 1511, and 
1 CONT known to have been alive in 1533;
1 CONT 4) a daughter, given name unknown, married to Oluf Tuesen. (The word 
1 CONT "fr?nde" can mean either "friend" or "relative".)
1 CONT Dr. Zarthmann attributes Gunhild Uf to be the "datterdatter" 
1 CONT (daughter's daughter) of Otte Pedersen Uf, thus making her the 
1 CONT daugher of Oluf Tuesen and the unkown Uf daughter.  While in "1000 
1 CONT Aner til en  Skovg¯rdsslgt", by Edvard Skovgaard, Gunhild Uf is 
1 CONT attributed to be the daughter of Anders Uf and Anne Sevidsdatter of 
1 CONT St. Kannikeg¯rd in Bodilsker parish; in 1449, as a wedding present 
1 CONT from her father Sevid Nielsen, they were given the ownership of St. 
1 CONT Kannikeg¯rd.  This would mean that Edvard Skovgaard's Anders Uf must 
1 CONT have been at least 25 years old in 1449 (25 was the age at which a 
1 CONT man could own land in that era), and thus born before 1426.  If he 
1 CONT was the same Anders Uf who was "fr?nde" to Anders Galen in 1511, then 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT he would have been at least 107 years old in 1533. It is probable 
1 CONT that Edvard Skovgaard has mistakenly attributed Anders Uf, the son of 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Otte Pedersen Uf, to be the same person as Anders Uf of St. 
1 CONT Kannikeg¯rd.  It has also been presented that Gunhild Uf and her  
1 CONT brothers are the children of Otte Pedersen Uf's son Anders Uf, rather 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT than that of his daughter and her husband Oluf Tuesen. Edvard 
1 CONT Skovgaard has lain out an ancestry for Anders Uf, of St. Kannikeg¯rd, 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT that leads back to the first King of a united Danmark: "Gorm den 
1 CONT Gamle", or Gorm the Elder, and his wife Thyra Dannebod.  History says 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT that Thyra was the descendant of a long line of Kings in England, 
1 CONT dating back to 400 A.D.  It is an accepted fact that the present 
1 CONT Queen of Danmark, Margrethe II, is a descendant of King Gorm; that 
1 CONT all of the Danish royalty since then have numbered among his 
1 CONT descendants. And thus through inter-marriage many of Europe's royal 
1 CONT families, including the British, are his descendants. Edvard 
1 CONT Skovgaard states that Anders Uf was the son of Otte Pedersen Uf (?-
1 CONT after 1497) and ??? Galen.  That  ??? Galen was the daughter of Tuve 
1 CONT (Tue) Galen (before 1320-after 1400) of N?sbyholm in Sk¯ne, who in 
1 CONT 1345 was made a Knight (Ridder), in 1364 Marschall, and from 1376-83 
1 CONT was "G?ldker" (Treasurer?); he married Ingefred Pedersdatter Urup on 
1 CONT April 15, 1345.  She was the daughter of Peder Holmgersen Urup (?-
1 CONT 1378),  who is a decendant of Cecilie - a daughter of King Knud 
1 CONT (Canute) the Holy (who was a gr-gr-gr-grandson of Gorm the Elder).  
1 CONT Edvard Skovgaard states  they had five children. While in the 
1 CONT book "Nachkommen Gorms des Alten" (Descendants of Gorm)  by Siegfried 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto Brenner (published in 1978 by the Danish Genealogy Society), 
1 CONT Tuve Galen is said to be a descendant of Gorm, it does not  list ??? 
1 CONT Galen.  He is recorded in that book as having four children: 
1 CONT Anders Galen (?-after 1399); Peder Galen (?-after 1400); Ingefred 
1 CONT Tuvesdatter Galen (?-1396) married to Johannes Absalonsen Ulfeldt (?-
1 CONT 1396); and ??? Tuvesdatter Galen married to Conrad Isenvorde 
1 CONT (Isenfurt) (?-1396/97), esquire in Sk¯ne.  It does NOT list a fifth 
1 CONT child: the un-named Galen daughter married to Otte Pedersen Uf. I 
1 CONT have not included Edvard Skovgaard's lineage of the Uf family in this 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT database for the simple reason I found the dates and connections to 
1 CONT be rather suspect.  Working with those dates that were provided I 
1 CONT found  that the un-named Galen woman must have been born between 1345 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT and 1380 (at the very latest!).  As Edvard Skovgaard's Anders Uf was 
1 CONT alive in 1533, it is doubtful that he was born before 1426.  Given a 
1 CONT limit to a woman's child-bearing years, by this calculation she could 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT not (by any stretch of the imagination) have given birth to a child 
1 CONT past 1430.  This would mean that Anders Uf would have had to have 
1 CONT lived to at least 105 years old, and have been fathering children at 
1 CONT 70-80 years old.  Also, since Otte Pedersen Uf was still alive in 
1 CONT 1497, it is doubtful he was born before 1400.  Meaning that Otte 
1 CONT Pedersen Uf, at 20-30, would have been  married to woman at least 20-
1 CONT 30 years older than himself.  This whole senario is remotely 
1 CONT possible, but highly unlikely! The Anders Uf of St. Kannikeg¯rd was a 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT nobleman (adelsmand) and is described as a "Storbonde"; I am not sure 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT of the word's exact meaning, however: stor=great and bonde=farmer, so 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT I suspect it means he was the holder of large amounts of land farmed 
1 CONT by "vornedskabe" peasants.  In the middle-ages the peasants had been 
1 CONT declared "vornedskabe" (bound), this meant that the descendants of 
1 CONT tenured peasants could be forced to stay on the "vornedegaard" they 
1 CONT were working, and it gave the owner of the farms the right to recall 
1 CONT a dead peasant's heir to take over working the land. By the 1500s 
1 CONT those tenured peasants who lived on manor-owned farms  worked off a 
1 CONT portion of their taxes by service in the manor's fields. That said, 
1 CONT it is still possible that the Uf-family of Bornholm is descended from 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Gorm; being as that they were of minor Danish nobility, and on 
1 CONT account of the nobility's tendancy to inter-marry amongst themselves. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT They seem to have been connected to families, (i.e. the Galen-
1 CONT family) known to be descended from Gorm; however, the possible 
1 CONT connection is far from certain and will probably remain that way! The 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT first member of the noble Uf-family to appear in Bornholm's 
1 CONT historical records was Hans Uf, who was appointed commander of 
1 CONT Hammershus fortress by his mother's brother Niels Jonsen, who was 
1 CONT Archbishop of Lund from 1361-1379.  During a long winter stay at the 
1 CONT fortress the Archbishop died on February 5, 1379.  Two days 
1 CONT previously he had written his testament, wherein he left to Hans Uf 
1 CONT his large gilded silver-horn and two silver cups, also 40 Marks in 
1 CONT silver to cover expenses for flour, malt, and other items used during 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT his stay.  As well he received the rights to six dwellings in Listed 
1 CONT fishing-village, which he had had built for the Archbishop, and which 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT brought in 6 Marks silver in yearly rent. During Hans Uf's long term 
1 CONT of office he acquired land in the region north-west of Svaneke.  The 
1 CONT Archbishop also thought of Hans Uf's daughter Cecilie, leaving her a 
1 CONT gold ring.  She was later married three times, all of her husbands 
1 CONT were noblemen from Sk¯ne. She outlived them all and in 1427 she 
1 CONT bestowed a gilded silver-chalice to Nyker Church, which is  still in 
1 CONT use, it bears the Uf-family coat-of-arms - with its characteristic 
1 CONT truss (sparren) - and the inscription: "Madame Cecilie gave this 
1 CONT chalice to All-Saints Church, pray for my soul."  This indicates that 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT the Uf-family had a home-farm in Nyker parish.
